# Worked solutions, chapter 2 (time response analysis), part 1: 2.1 - 2.53
S = {
"2.1": ("A", "Overshoots and undershoots of an underdamped second-order step response.", "Successive extrema of the step response occur at $t=n\\pi/\\omega_d$ with magnitude $e^{-n\\xi\\pi/\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}$. Odd $n$ are overshoots and even $n$ are undershoots.\n\nThe largest undershoot is $n=2$: $e^{-2\\xi\\pi/\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}$, so (A) is true. (R) is the general formula that gives (A).\n\nBoth true and (R) explains (A): **(a)**."),
"2.2": ("A", "Critical damping needs $\\xi=1$, i.e. a repeated real pole.", "Compare with $s^2+2\\xi\\omega_ns+\\omega_n^2$.\n$H_1$: $\\omega_n=2$, $2\\xi\\omega_n=4\\Rightarrow\\xi=1$.\n$H_2$: $\\xi=0.75$; $H_3$: $\\xi=0.5$; $H_4$: $\\xi=0.25$.\n\nCritically damped is $H_1$: **(a)**."),
"2.3": ("C", "Settling time of a second-order system.", "The envelope $e^{-\\xi\\omega_nt}$ falls to 2% at $\\xi\\omega_nt\\approx4$. So $t_s=\\dfrac{4}{\\xi\\omega_n}$ (2%) and $\\dfrac{3}{\\xi\\omega_n}$ (5%): **(c)**."),
"2.4": ("D", "Non-interacting cascade of two identical first-order lags.", "$G(s)=\\left(\\dfrac1{\\tau s+1}\\right)^2$ has a repeated real pole at $-1/\\tau$. That is $\\xi=1$, so the step response is critically damped: **(d)**."),
"2.5": ("A", "$Y=H\\cdot X$, then inverse Laplace transform.", "$X(s)=\\dfrac1{s+1}$ and $H(s)=\\dfrac{s+1}{(s+1)^2+1}$.\n$Y(s)=\\dfrac{1}{(s+1)^2+1}\\Rightarrow y(t)=e^{-t}\\sin t\\,u(t)$: **(a)**."),
"2.6": ("B", "Position error constant $K_p=\\lim_{s\\to0}G(s)$; $e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{1+K_p}$.", "$K_p=\\dfrac{10}{50}=0.2$, so the system is type 0.\n$e_{ss}=\\dfrac{1}{1+0.2}=0.833$: **(b)**."),
"2.7": ("C", "Step response $\\to T(s)=sC(s)$.", "$C(s)=\\dfrac1s-\\dfrac{10}{s+1}=\\dfrac{1-9s}{s(s+1)}$.\n$T(s)=sC(s)=\\dfrac{1-9s}{s+1}$: **(c)**."),
"2.8": (None, "Order reduction by neglecting a fast (non-dominant) pole.", "Dropping a non-dominant pole removes a small lag, so with the DC gain preserved the reduced model responds somewhat faster to a step. This points to a smaller rise time, but the options say \"for any input\" and \"depending on the input\", and rise time is only defined for a step. The wording is ambiguous, so no answer key has been set for this question."),
"2.9": ("B", "Pole location decides the response: $\\sigma<0$ decays, $\\sigma=0$ sustains, $\\sigma>0$ grows.", "- **A**: all poles real, two in the right half-plane $\\Rightarrow$ non-oscillatory, unbounded response: **4**.\n- **B**: poles at $\\pm j\\omega$ $\\Rightarrow$ sustained oscillation, constant amplitude: **3**.\n- **C**: complex pair in the right half-plane $\\Rightarrow$ growing oscillation: **2**.\n- **D**: complex pair in the left half-plane $\\Rightarrow$ decaying oscillation: **1**.\n\nA–4, B–3, C–2, D–1: **(b)**."),
"2.10": ("A", "Impulse response of $1/s$.", "A single pole at the origin gives $G(s)=\\dfrac Ks$, so $g(t)=Ku(t)$, a constant: **(a)**."),
"2.11": ("B", "Steady-state error sensitivity: $S_x^{e}=\\dfrac{x}{e}\\dfrac{\\partial e}{\\partial x}$.", "For a ramp, $K_v=\\lim_{s\\to0}sG=\\dfrac Ka$, so $e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{K_v}=\\dfrac aK$.\n$S_K=\\dfrac{K}{e}\\cdot\\left(-\\dfrac a{K^2}\\right)=-1$ and $S_a=\\dfrac ae\\cdot\\dfrac1K=+1$.\n\n$(-1,\\ 1)$: **(b)**."),
"2.12": ("B", "$H(s)=sY(s)$ for a unit step input.", "$Y(s)=\\mathcal L\\{t^2e^{-t}\\}=\\dfrac{2}{(s+1)^3}$.\n$H(s)=sY(s)=\\dfrac{2s}{(s+1)^3}$: **(b)**."),
"2.13": ("A", "Ramp error by system type: $e_{ss}=\\infty,\\ 1/K_v,\\ 0$ for types 0, 1, $\\ge2$.", "- Type 0: the error grows without bound $\\Rightarrow\\infty$ (2).\n- Type 1: $e_{ss}=1/K$ (4).\n- Type 2: $0$ (3).\n- Type 3: $0$ (3).\n\nA–2, B–4, C–3, D–3: **(a)**."),
"2.14": ("B", "Definition of steady-state error.", "The error that remains after the transient has died out, i.e. for a large observation time, is the steady-state error: **(b)**."),
"2.15": ("D", "First-order impulse response $Ke^{-\\alpha t}$ falls to $0.37K$ at $t=1/\\alpha$.", "$g(t)=Ke^{-\\alpha t}$. At $t=1/\\alpha$ it equals $Ke^{-1}=0.37K$.\nThe figure shows the $0.37K$ point at $t_2$, so $t_2=1/\\alpha$ and $\\alpha=\\dfrac1{t_2}$: **(d)**."),
"2.16": ("A", "Discriminant of the characteristic equation.", "$1+G=0\\Rightarrow s^2+18s+80=(s+8)(s+10)=0$.\nThe roots are real and distinct ($\\xi=9/\\sqrt{80}\\approx1.006>1$), so the step response is overdamped: **(a)**.\n\nIt is very close to critical damping, but not equal to it."),
"2.17": ("B", "What feedback does.", "1. Reduces sensitivity to parameter variation and disturbances: true.\n2. Moves the poles, so it changes the time constant: true.\n3. Feedback does not increase the loop gain, it uses the loop gain: false.\n\nOnly 1 and 2: **(b)**."),
"2.18": ("C", "Critical damping is a repeated real negative root.", "$\\xi=1$ gives $s=-\\omega_n$ twice: negative, real and equal: **(c)**."),
"2.19": ("A", "Pole-zero cancellation and DC gain.", "From the step response the poles are at $-1$ and $-3$, so $a,b=1,3$.\nThe response to $e^{-2t}u(t)$ has no $e^{-2t}$ term, so the zero must cancel the input pole: $c=2$.\nThe step response's constant term is $H(0)=\\dfrac{Hc}{ab}=\\dfrac{2H}{3}=2\\Rightarrow H=3$.\n\n$c=2,\\ H=3$: **(a)**."),
"2.20": ("C", "Disturbance rejection: raise the gain before the disturbance enters.", "With $R=0$: $\\dfrac{C}{D}=\\dfrac{G_2}{1+G_1G_2}$. At DC with large $G_1G_2$ this is about $\\dfrac1{G_1}$.\nRaising only $G_2$ cannot reduce this below $1/G_1$, but raising the DC gain of $G_1$ (the block *ahead* of the disturbance) drives the error towards zero: **(c)**."),
"2.21": ("C", "Extrema of the step response occur at $t=n\\pi/\\omega_d$.", "$\\omega_d=\\omega_n\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}$. The overshoots are at odd $n$: the first peak is at $\\pi/\\omega_d$ and the second peak at $3\\pi/\\omega_d$. (The minima in between at even $n$ are undershoots.)\n\n$t=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{\\omega_n\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}$: **(c)**."),
"2.22": ("B", "Negative feedback reduces sensitivity.", "$S=\\dfrac1{1+GH}<1$ for $|1+GH|>1$, so the sensitivity to parameter variations decreases: **(b)**."),
"2.23": ("C", "Negative damping puts the poles in the right half-plane.", "Underdamped means complex-conjugate poles. Negative damping makes the real part $-\\xi\\omega_n$ positive, so the poles are complex conjugates in the right half-plane: **(c)**."),
"2.24": ("C", "Classify by $\\xi=\\dfrac{b}{2\\sqrt c}$ for $s^2+bs+c$.", "- A: $\\omega_n=20,\\ \\xi=12/40=0.3$: underdamped (3).\n- B: $\\omega_n=30,\\ \\xi=90/60=1.5$: overdamped (4).\n- C: $\\omega_n=15,\\ \\xi=30/30=1$: critically damped (2).\n- D: $\\xi=0$: undamped (1).\n\nA–3, B–4, C–2, D–1: **(c)**."),
"2.25": ("B", "Match $s^2+2\\xi\\omega_ns+\\omega_n^2$.", "$1+G=0\\Rightarrow s^2+4s+K$. So $\\omega_n=\\sqrt K$ and $2\\xi\\omega_n=4$.\n$\\xi=\\dfrac2{\\sqrt K}=0.5\\Rightarrow\\sqrt K=4\\Rightarrow K=16$: **(b)**."),
"2.26": ("A", "Ramp error constant $K_v=\\lim_{s\\to0}sG$.", "$K_v=\\lim_{s\\to0}\\dfrac{s}{s(s+1)}=1$.\n$e_{ss}=\\dfrac1{K_v}=1$: **(a)**."),
"2.27": ("B", "Peak overshoot gives $\\xi$; then $K=\\omega_n^2$.", "$M_p=e^{-\\pi\\xi/\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}=0.5\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{\\pi\\xi}{\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}=\\ln2=0.693\\Rightarrow\\xi\\approx0.2155$.\nCharacteristic equation $s^2+s+K$: $2\\xi\\omega_n=1\\Rightarrow\\omega_n=2.32$.\n$K=\\omega_n^2\\approx5.4\\approx5.3$: **(b)**."),
"2.28": ("B", "First-order time constant.", "1. A transient-response specification: true.\n2. $\\tau=1/|p|$, the reciprocal of the pole on the real axis: true.\n3. Accuracy relates to steady-state error, not $\\tau$: false.\n4. A smaller $\\tau$ means a faster response: true.\n\nOnly 1, 2 and 4: **(b)**."),
"2.29": ("D", "Compare with the critically damped step response.", "$C(s)=\\dfrac{25}{s(s+5)^2}$, so $\\omega_n=5$ rad/s and $\\xi=1$ (statements 1 and 2 true).\nImpulse response $=\\dfrac{d}{dt}$(step response) $=5e^{-5t}-5e^{-5t}+25te^{-5t}=25te^{-5t}$ (statement 3 true).\n\nAll three: **(d)**."),
"2.30": ("B", "Stability from pole locations.", "$C(s)=\\dfrac1s-\\dfrac1{s+1}-\\dfrac1{(s+1)^2}=\\dfrac{1}{s(s+1)^2}$, so $T(s)=\\dfrac1{(s+1)^2}$.\nBoth poles are at $-1$ (left half-plane), so the system is stable: **(b)**."),
"2.31": ("A", "Impulse response is the derivative of the step response.", "The step response is natural response + a constant particular term. Differentiating removes the constant and only rescales the exponential terms, so the impulse response contains only natural-response terms. (A) is true and (R) is the reason.\n\n**(a)**."),
"2.32": ("B", "Forced response for constant input.", "For $x=k$ the forced (steady) response has $\\dot y=0$: $3y=k\\Rightarrow y=\\dfrac k3$: **(b)**."),
"2.33": ("B", "Steady-state error falls as the error constants rise.", "$e_{ss}\\propto\\dfrac1{1+K}$ or $\\dfrac1{K_v}$, so increasing the system gain reduces it: **(b)**. Reducing the type or the error constant would increase it."),
"2.34": ("D", "Normalise to standard form.", "$2\\ddot y+4\\dot y+8y=8x\\Rightarrow\\ddot y+2\\dot y+4y=4x$.\n$\\omega_n^2=4\\Rightarrow\\omega_n=2$ and $2\\xi\\omega_n=2\\Rightarrow\\xi=0.5$: **(d)**."),
"2.35": ("B", "Poles for $\\xi=0$.", "$s^2+\\omega_n^2=0\\Rightarrow s=\\pm j\\omega_n$: purely imaginary: **(b)**."),
"2.36": ("C", "Type 2 system with a ramp input.", "$G=\\dfrac{10}{s^2(s+4)}$ is type 2, so $K_v=\\infty$ and the ramp error is zero (for any ramp slope): **(c)**."),
"2.37": ("D", "Check $\\xi$ from the characteristic equation.", "$4s^2+6s+1=0\\Rightarrow s^2+1.5s+0.25=0$.\n$\\omega_n=0.5$ rad/s (so (R) is true), and $2\\xi\\omega_n=1.5\\Rightarrow\\xi=1.5>1$: overdamped, so (A) is false.\n\n**(d)**."),
"2.38": ("B", "DC gain of a second-order all-pole model.", "Poles at $-1\\pm j1$, no finite zeros: $H(s)=\\dfrac{1}{(s+1)^2+1}=\\dfrac1{s^2+2s+2}$ (taking a numerator of 1).\nSteady-state value for a unit step $=H(0)=\\dfrac12=0.5$: **(b)**."),
"2.39": ("C", "Peak time $t_p=\\pi/\\omega_d$.", "$\\omega_n=4,\\ 2\\xi\\omega_n=4\\Rightarrow\\xi=0.5$.\n$\\omega_d=4\\sqrt{1-0.25}=2\\sqrt3$.\n$t_p=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2\\sqrt3}$ s: **(c)**."),
"2.40": ("A", "Dominant poles are the ones closest to the $j\\omega$ axis.", "The poles at $-0.5$ and $-1$ are slow; those at $-5$ and $-10$ decay at least 5 times faster. The zeros at $-6$ and $-8$ are far away and have little influence on the slow part.\nThe response is closely approximated by $p_1$ and $p_2$: **(a)**."),
"2.41": ("D", "Sustained oscillation has no steady-state value.", "$f(t)=\\sin\\omega t$, which keeps oscillating between $-1$ and $+1$, so there is no steady-state limit. The final value theorem fails because the poles are on the imaginary axis.\n\nThe value stays between $-1$ and $+1$: **(d)**."),
"2.42": ("D", "$t_s=4/(\\xi\\omega_n)$ for the 2% band.", "$1+G=0\\Rightarrow s^2+0.4s+4=0$, so $\\omega_n=2$ and $\\xi\\omega_n=0.2$.\n$t_s=\\dfrac4{0.2}=20$ s: **(d)**."),
"2.43": ("D", "An underdamped response is a decaying oscillation.", "(a) and (b) are not oscillatory. (c) is an oscillation whose envelope *grows*, which is an unstable system. (d) is an oscillation with a decaying envelope, i.e. a damped oscillation: **(d)**."),
"2.44": ("B", "Trade-off between overshoot and speed.", "Very small $\\xi$ gives a lightly damped, oscillatory response. $\\xi>1$ gives a sluggish one. A good compromise between stability and speed is $\\xi\\approx0.4$ to $0.7$: **(b)**."),
"2.45": ("B", "Effects of integral and derivative action.", "(A) Integral action raises the type, so steady-state error falls: true.\n(R) Derivative action adds damping, which reduces overshoot: true.\nThe two are unrelated, so (R) does not explain (A): **(b)**."),
"2.46": (None, "BIBO stability.", "(R) is the condition for stability: roots in the left half-plane and decaying initial-condition response. (A) holds only for *stable* linear systems, since a linear integrator gives an unbounded output for a bounded step. Whether the intended reading of (A) is \"every linear system\" or \"a stable linear system\" is unclear, so no answer key has been set."),
"2.47": ("B", "Closed-loop transfer function and final value.", "Forward gain $\\dfrac{10}{s+1}$ with feedback $\\dfrac1s$:\n$T=\\dfrac{10/(s+1)}{1+10/[s(s+1)]}=\\dfrac{10s}{s^2+s+10}$.\nUnit step: $C(s)=\\dfrac{10}{s^2+s+10}$, and the final value is $\\lim_{s\\to0}sC(s)=0$: **(b)**."),
"2.48": ("B", "System type counts the integrators in $G(s)H(s)$.", "$G(s)H(s)=\\dfrac{2}{s^2+2s+2}\\cdot\\dfrac1s=\\dfrac{2}{s(s^2+2s+2)}$.\nOne pole at the origin: type 1: **(b)**."),
"2.49": ("B", "First-order form $\\tau\\dot x+x=Kf$.", "$40\\dot x+2x=f\\Rightarrow20\\dot x+x=\\tfrac12f$. So $\\tau=20$: **(b)**."),
"2.50": ("B", "Settling time and damping ratio fix the pole location.", "$t_s(5\\%)=\\dfrac{3}{\\xi\\omega_n}=0.6\\Rightarrow\\xi\\omega_n=5$.\n$\\xi=0.707\\Rightarrow\\omega_n=7.07$, so $\\omega_d=\\omega_n\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}=5$.\nPoles: $-5\\pm j5$: **(b)**."),
"2.51": ("C", "Match $s^2+2\\xi\\omega_ns+\\omega_n^2$.", "$s^2+6s+K=0\\Rightarrow\\omega_n=\\sqrt K,\\ 2\\xi\\omega_n=6$.\n$\\xi=\\dfrac3{\\sqrt K}=0.75\\Rightarrow\\sqrt K=4\\Rightarrow K=16$: **(c)**."),
"2.52": ("B", "Impulse response of an underdamped second-order system.", "The impulse response $\\propto e^{-\\xi\\omega_nt}\\sin\\omega_dt$ rings at the system's own (natural) oscillation frequency, so (I) holds in the loose sense. (II) is true: an impulse has a flat spectrum. The impulse excites every mode, but (II) alone does not determine which frequency the system rings at.\n\nBoth true, (II) not the explanation: **(b)**."),
"2.53": ("A", "$t_r\\approx\\dfrac{1.8}{\\omega_n}$ and bandwidth $\\propto\\omega_n$.", "In a prototype second-order system $t_r\\propto1/\\omega_n$ and bandwidth $\\propto\\omega_n$, so they are inversely proportional (I). Increasing $\\omega_n$ raises the bandwidth and lowers $t_r$ (II), which is the reason for (I).\n\n**(a)**."),
}
